course: dictionary
lesson: 8558
title: The shoulder blade
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The scapula is the bone you call your shoulder blade. It is the flat, triangle-shaped bone at the top of your back. Scapula is a Latin word, so it looks strange, but you already know the shape of the thing it names.
explain: Context gives you the clue. «The shoulder blade, or scapula, is a flat triangular bone» tells you the two names mean the same thing. The word «or» points to a second name for the same bone.
explain: The scapula joins your arm bone, the humerus, to your collarbone, the clavicle. It glides on your back, so «the arm can move widely». That is why you can lift, throw and reach in so many directions.
fact: The scapula is the shoulder blade.
fact: The scapula is a flat, triangle-shaped bone at the top of the back.
fact: Scapula is pronounced SKAP-yoo-la. The plural is scapulae or scapulas.
fact: The scapula joins the humerus (arm bone) to the clavicle (collarbone).
fact: «Scapula» is a noun, a naming word for a bone.
exercise: «The shoulder blade, or scapula, is a flat triangular bone.» What is another name for the shoulder blade? | Clavicle | *Scapula | Humerus | Femur
exercise: Which arm bone does the scapula join? | *Humerus | Tibia | Femur | Skull
exercise: What shape is the scapula? | Square like a box | Long and thin like a stick | Round like a ball | *Flat and triangular
test: Where on your body will you find the scapula? | In your stomach | In your leg | *At the top of your back | In your head
test: Which two bones does the scapula join? | *Humerus and clavicle | Clavicle and skull | Humerus and femur | Femur and tibia
exam: «The scapula glides on the back so the arm can move widely.» Why is this useful? | *It lets the arm move in many directions | It makes the back straight | It protects the stomach | It keeps the head still
